<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hàm Nghi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hàm Nghi was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of the Nguyễn dynasty from 1884 to 1885. Enthroned at thirteen, he fled the capital Huế in July 1885 with the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> after a failed attack on the French, and his name was used to rally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vuong">Cần Vương</a> resistance movement. Betrayed and captured in 1888, he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, where he lived as a painter until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ham-nghi.jpg" alt="Hàm Nghi" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vua_H%C3%A0m_Nghi_n%C4%83m_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hàm Nghi was born Nguyễn Phúc Ưng Lịch in Huế, the fifth son of Prince Nguyễn Phúc Hồng Cai and brother of Emperor Kiến Phúc. After Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tu-duc">Tự Đức</a> died in July 1883, the regents Nguyễn Văn Tường and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> held power over the choice of emperors. Three emperors had already been removed or had died. The regents wanted a young ruler who shared their anti-French stance, and they chose Ưng Lịch, who had lived in poverty and simplicity with his mother rather than being educated in the palace. He was enthroned in the Thái Hòa Hall on 2 August 1884 with the reign title Hàm Nghi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Resident Superior, Pierre Paul Rheinart, sent troops into Huế to force the court to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s permission after the fact. The court's request, written in chữ Nôm, was refused until it was rewritten in Chinese characters. At the investiture on 17 August 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> allowed only three French envoys through the Noon Gate, which was traditionally reserved for the emperor.</p><p>On 4 July 1885 an insurrection against the French broke out in the emperor's name, and after the French stormed the palace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> took Hàm Nghi into hiding. The French replaced him with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-khanh">Đồng Khánh</a>. Hàm Nghi withdrew into the hills around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> with Tôn Thất Thuyết's force, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vuong">Cần Vương</a> edict called scholars and patriots to resist French rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1888 Hàm Nghi was betrayed by Trương Quang Ngọc, the head of his Muong guards, and captured while hiding near the Nai river. He was exiled to French Algeria, arriving on 13 January 1889, and was deposed and titled Duke Ưng Lịch. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> he studied under the painter Marius Reynaud, and his painting moved from Orientalism towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a> and Post-Impressionism. He signed his works Tử Xuân. The art historian Amandine Dabat, his descendant, argues that he was the first modern Vietnamese painter. He died on 14 January 1944 and lies in a simple grave at Thonac in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a>. Under French pressure the Nguyễn dynasty gave him no temple name. Vietnamese history counts him among three patriotic emperors of the colonial period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hàm Nghi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet the dynasty also produced resistance: the Can Vuong (‘Aid the King’) movement launched in the name of the boy emperor <b>Ham Nghi</b> in 1885, and the emperors Thanh Thai and Duy Tan, deposed and exiled by the French in 1907 and 1916, became patriotic symbols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>France deposed emperors who resisted, including <b>Ham Nghi</b>, who led the Can Vuong movement from 1885, and later Thanh Thai and Duy Tan.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-annam">French protectorate of Annam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in El Biar, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Huế on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hàm Nghi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3989 6093h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3989 6093h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
